\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Modular data transform covariantly, but are not fixed by covariance.} Let $H$ be standard and $V$ unitary. On $VH+iVH$, the Tomita operator satisfies $S_{VH}=VS_HV^*$, directly from $S_H(h+ik)=h-ik$. Therefore $\Delta_{VH}=V\Delta_HV^*$. In particular, any unitary preserving $H$ commutes with its modular group, but need not equal that group at a rescaled parameter.

\paragraph{An explicit one-subspace test.} Take $H=\mathbb R^2\subset\mathbb C^2$. Its Tomita operator is complex conjugation, so $\Delta_H=I$. The nontrivial real rotations $V_t=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}\cos t&-\sin t\\\sin t&\cos t\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ preserve $H$ and are unitary on $\mathbb C^2$, yet cannot coincide with the trivial modular group. Thus covariance of a subspace under a one-parameter action gives no modular identification by itself.

\paragraph{The net-level obstruction.} A prospective $U'$ is constrained on every wedge boost by $U'(\Lambda_W(-2\pi t))=\Delta_{H(W)}^{it}$ in one sign convention. These separately defined groups must satisfy all joint Lorentz and translation relations, preserve the same double-cone net and have positive translation energy. The toy example is not a four-dimensional local net and does not disprove existence of such a replacement representation. The first gap is proving those compatibility relations for the actual family of wedge modular operators. Replacing the net or Hilbert space would evade the source target and is not done.

